# Mental Models (Learning Context)

In the context of Senge's framework, mental models are deeply ingrained assumptions that shape our perceptions and decisions in every aspect of life. Examining and questioning these models is essential for developing a systemic perspective and improving how individuals and teams make sense of complex situations.

## Core Ideas

- Mental models are deeply ingrained assumptions influencing perception and decision-making
- They operate in every aspect of life, often unconsciously
- Examining mental models reveals hidden biases and limitations in thinking
- Challenging mental models opens pathways to new ways of understanding complex problems

## Connection to Broader Mental Model Work

This concept connects to existing wiki coverage of [[biases]] and [[decision-making-frameworks]], extending those ideas into the organizational learning dimension. Where individual cognitive biases affect personal decisions, organizational mental models shape collective understanding and strategy.

